            <ArticleTitle>Conservative endodontic management of a fused tooth: A case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>398-400</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/ger.12266</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To report a conservative treatment of a rare developmental anomaly.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE REPORT" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 73-year-old patient with previously initiated therapy and acute apical abscess of a maxillary right central incisor fused with the supernumerary tooth sought treatment. The conservative approach included nonsurgical root canal treatment and composite restoration.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This case illustrates the importance of an individual approach when treating anomalous teeth. Priorities in pain and infection management to properly and functionally restore teeth should be unaffected by age.</AbstractText>
